Let $\mu,
u$ be two probability measures over $\{\pm1\}^{n}$, and suppose that for some $c > 1$ (possibly depending on $n$), $ rac{1}{c} \leq  rac{
u(\sigma)}{\mu(\sigma)} \leq c$ uniformly over all $\sigma \in \{\pm1\}^{n}$.  egin{enumerate} \item Suppose Glauber dynamics for $\mu$ has MLSI constant at least $\MLSI$. Then, Glauber dynamics for $
u$ has MLSI constant at least $\MLSI / c^2$. \item For any function $f$, \[  rac{1}{c} \cdot \Var_{\mu}[f] \le \Var_{
u}[f] \le c \cdot \Var_{\mu}[f]. \] \end{enumerate} Also note that if $
u \propto e^{-f}$ and $\mu \propto e^{-g}$, and $\|f-g\|_{\infty} \le \wt{c}$, then $e^{-2\wt{c}} \le  rac{
u(\sigma)}{\mu(\sigma)} \le e^{2\wt{c}}$.

Let $H$ be a $(\Delta,D)$-pseudorandom tree with respect to $S\subseteq V(H)$. Let $A$ be the adjacency matrix of $H$. If $J$ is a collection of interactions supported on the edges of $H$ with values in $ racks*{0,  rac{\gamma}{\sqrt{D}}}$ for some $\gamma \in [0,1)$, then  egin{align*} 
orm*{\Cov\parens*{\mu_{J,0}}_S} &\le  rac{1}{(1-\gamma)^2} \ 
orm*{\Cov\parens*{\mu_{J,0}}} &\le  rac{1}{(1-\gamma)^2}\cdot rac{\Delta}{D} \ 
orm*{\Cov\left(\mu_{J,0}ight) \cdot A} &\le  rac{1}{(1-\gamma)^2} \cdot  rac{2\Delta}{\sqrt{D}} \mper \end{align*}

We start with the first two bounds, using the following explicit formula for the covariance matrix. \[ \Cov\parens*{\mu_{J,0}} = \sum_{\ell\ge 0} X^{(\ell)}, \] where $X^{(\ell)}_{i,j}$ is $0$ if there is no length-$\ell$ path between $i$ and $j$ in $H$, and is otherwise equal to $\prod_{e\in P(i,j)} 	anh(J_e)$ where $P(i,j)$ is the unique such path. For $R\subseteq V(H)$, will use the bound:  egin{equation} 
orm*{\Cov\parens*{\mu_{J,0}}_R} \le \sum_{\ell\ge 0} 
orm*{X^{(\ell)}_R}. \end{equation} Consider the matrix $Y^{(\ell)}$ obtained by replacing each nonzero entry of $X^{(\ell)}$ with $\parens*{ rac{\gamma}{\sqrt{D}}}^{\ell}$. Since the interactions are all nonnegative, $X^{(\ell)}$ is a nonnegative matrix, and $Y^{(\ell)}$ is entrywise at least $X^{(\ell)}$ since $	anh(x) \le x$ for $x \ge 0$. Consequently,  egin{equation} 
orm*{X^{(\ell)}_R} \le 
orm*{Y^{(\ell)}_R}. \end{equation} \Cref{lem:trace-method-best-method} now implies that \[ 
orm*{Y^{(\ell)}_R} \le \left(  rac{\gamma}{\sqrt{D}} ight)^{\ell} \cdot D^{\ell/2} \cdot (\ell+1) \cdot \left( rac{\Delta_R}{D}ight) = \gamma^{\ell} \cdot (\ell+1) \cdot \left( rac{\Delta_R}{D}ight). \] Therefore, \[ 
orm*{\Cov\parens*{\mu_{J,0}}_R} \le  rac{\Delta_R}{D} \cdot \sum_{\ell \ge 0} (\ell+1) \gamma^{\ell} \le  rac{1}{(1-\gamma)^2}\cdot rac{\Delta_R}{D}\mcom \] which recovers the desired bounds by plugging in $\Delta_R = \Delta$ for $R = V(H)$, and $\Delta_R = D$ for $R=S$.\ For the third inequality, we again have \[ \Cov\left(\mu_{J,0}ight)\cdot A = \sum_{\ell \ge 0} 
orm*{X^{(\ell)} \cdot A}. \] Again, the non-negativity of $X^{(\ell)}$ and $A$ implies that \[ 
orm*{X^{(\ell)} \cdot A} \le 
orm*{Y^{(\ell)} \cdot A}. \] The third bound in \Cref{lem:trace-method-best-method} now implies that  egin{align*} 
orm*{Y^{(\ell)} \cdot A} &\le \left( rac{\gamma}{\sqrt{D}}ight)^{\ell} D^{(\ell+1)/2} \cdot (\ell+1) \cdot 2 \cdot \left(  rac{\Delta}{D} ight) \ &= \gamma^{\ell} \cdot (\ell+1) \cdot 2 \cdot  rac{\Delta}{\sqrt{D}}. \end{align*} Therefore, \[ 
orm*{\Cov\left(\mu_{J,0}ight) \cdot A} \le \sum_{\ell \ge 0} \gamma^{\ell} \cdot (\ell+1) \cdot 2 \cdot  rac{\Delta}{\sqrt{D}} \le  rac{1}{(1-\gamma)^2} \cdot  rac{2\Delta}{\sqrt{D}}, \] completing the proof.

Let $H$ be a tree as in the lemma statement, $A$ its adjacency matrix, and $J =  rac{\gamma}{\sqrt{D}}$ the corresponding interaction matrix. Also set $D_H$ be the (diagonal) degree matrix of $H$. To prove this, we shall once again use \Cref{lem:entropic-stability}. The control matrix will be of the form $C = (J+E)^{1/2} \psdge 0$ for a non-negative diagonal matrix $E$. Due to the ferromagneticity of the system, $C^2$ has all non-negative entries. At time $0 \le t \le 1$, $J_t$ will have the form $(1-t) J - tE$ -- in particular, at time $t=1$, $\mu_t$ is almost surely a product distribution so $\MLSI(\mu_t) = \Omega(1/n)$. In the context of \Cref{lem:entropic-stability}, our goal is to bound, for all external fields $h \in \R^n$, the operator norm  egin{equation} 
orm*{C \cdot \Cov\left( \mu_{(1-t)J,h} ight) \cdot C} \le 
orm*{\Cov\left( \mu_{(1-t)J,h} ight) \cdot C^2 }. \end{equation} By \Cref{cor:cov-ferro-external-psd} (using the matrix $C^2$), it suffices to bound the above for $h = 0$. For $h = 0$, we recall that if $A^{(\ell)}$ is the matrix where the $uv$-th entry is $1$ if $u$ and $v$ are distance exactly $\ell$ apart and $0$ otherwise, \[ \Cov\left( \mu_{(1-t)J,0} ight) \le \sum_{\ell \ge 0} \left(  rac{\gamma}{\sqrt{D}} ight)^{\ell} A^{(\ell)} \] in the sense that the matrix on the right is entry-wise at least the matrix on the left. In particular,  egin{equation} 
orm*{\Cov\left( \mu_{(1-t)J,h} ight) \cdot C^2 } \le 
orm*{ \sum_{\ell \ge 0} \left(  rac{\gamma}{\sqrt{D}} ight)^{\ell} A^{(\ell)} \cdot C^2 }. \end{equation} For ease of notation, denote $s_0 =  rac{\gamma}{\sqrt{D}}$. Recalling \Cref{def:bethe-hessian}, set \[ \BH(s) =  rac{(D_H - I) s^2 - As + I}{1-s^2}, \] so by \Cref{fact:bethe-inverse}, \[ \BH\left( s_0 ight)^{-1} = \sum_{\ell \ge 0} \left(  rac{\gamma}{\sqrt{D}} ight)^\ell A^{(\ell)}. \] We choose  egin{align} C = \left((1-s_0^2) \cdot \BH\left( - s_0 ight)ight)^{1/2} . \end{align} Now,  egin{align*} \sum_{\ell \ge 0} \left(  rac{\gamma}{\sqrt{D}} ight)^{\ell} A^{(\ell)} \cdot C^2 &= (1-s_0^2) \cdot \BH\left( s_0 ight)^{-1} \cdot \BH\left( -s_0 ight) \ &= (1-s_0^2) \cdot \BH(s_0)^{-1} \cdot \left( \BH(s_0) +  rac{2s_0}{1-s_0^2} \cdot A ight) \ &= (1-s_0^2) \cdot I + 2 s_0 \cdot \BH(s_0)^{-1} \cdot A. \end{align*} Using the third bound in \Cref{lem:tree-cov} with \eqref{eq:bethe-hessian-coincidence-1} and \eqref{eq:bethe-hessian-coincidence-2}, we get the desired claim that  egin{align*} 
orm*{C \cdot \Cov\left( \mu_{(1-t)J,h} ight) \cdot C} &\le 
orm*{ (1-s_0^2) \cdot I + 2 s_0 \cdot \BH(s_0)^{-1} \cdot A } \ &\le 1 +  rac{8}{(1-\gamma)^2} \cdot  rac{\Delta}{D}. \end{align*} Given that the product measure obtained at time $1$ satisfies an MLSI with constant $1/n$, it follows by \Cref{lem:entropic-stability,thm:anneal} that  egin{align*} \MLSI(\mu_{J,h}) &\ge  rac{1}{n} \cdot \exp\left( - \int_{0}^{1} \sup_{h \in \R^n} 
orm*{ C \cdot \Cov\left( \mu_{(1-t)J, h} ight) \cdot C } \,dt ight) \ &\ge  rac{1}{n} \cdot \exp\left( - \left( 1 +  rac{8}{(1-\gamma)^2} \cdot  rac{\Delta}{D} ight) ight) =  rac{1}{ne^{O(\Delta)}} \end{align*} as desired.

The \emph{Dobrushin influence matrix} of $
u$, denoted $\dobinf_{
u}$, is a $k	imes k$ matrix where: \[ \dobinf_{
u}[i,j] \coloneqq \max_{\substack{\sigma_{-i}, 	au_{-i} \ 	ext{that agree on coordinates }\ [k]\setminus \{i,j\} }} d_{\mathrm{TV}}\parens*{ 
u|\sigma_{-i}, 
u|	au_{-i} }\mper \]

Suppose for every tilt $\lambda$, the influence matrix satisfies: \[ 
orm*{\dobinf_{
u\ast\lambda}} \le \delta < 1. \] Then for every function $f:\calP	o\R_{> 0}$\mcom \[ \E_{i\sim[k]}\E_{ x_{-i}\sim
u_{-i}} \Ent_{
u_i}[f] \ge  rac{1}{k^{ rac{1}{1-\delta}}}\cdot\Ent_{
u}[f]\mper \]

In particular, for us $k = m + m'$ is the total number of connected components of $H$ (including isolated vertices). Indeed, due to \Cref{lem:parameter-dep,cor:dobrushin}, we have  egin{align*} 
orm{\dobinf_{
u}} &\le 8\alpha d^2 + \sqrt{8\alpha d^2} \ &\le \exp(-\Omega(\eps^3 d)) 	ag*{(\Cref{lem:component-size})}. \end{align*} We now have the following lemma from \cite{Hay06}.  egin{lemma}[\cite{Hay06}] If the influence matrix of a distribution $
u$ is bounded as $\|\calR_{
u}\| < 1-\eps$, the mixing time of Glauber dynamics is $O((n \log n)/\eps)$. \end{lemma} In conjunction with the previous equations, this implies an optimal mixing time bound for the block dynamics, and thus an $\Omega\left( rac{1}{k}ight)$ bound on $\PI(
u)$.

By \Cref{fact:var-cov}, if $v \in \R^n$ is a unit norm vector supported on $S \subseteq [n]$, then there is a positive constant $c > 0$ such that  egin{align*} \Vart_{x \sim \mu_{M_R, h'}}(\angles{v, x}) &\le c \sum_{C \in \cc(H)} \E_{x_{-C}} \Vart_{x \sim \mu_{M_C + E_C, h(x_{-C})}}(\angles{v_C, x_C}) 	ag*{(	ext{\Cref{lem:poincare}})}\ &\le c(1+o_n(1)) \sum_{C \in \cc(H)} \E_{x_{-C}} \Vart_{x \sim \mu_{M_C, h(x_{-C})}}(\angles{v_C, x_C}) 	ag*{(	ext{\Cref{lem:mlsi-bdd-density-relate}})} \ &\le c(1+o_n(1)) \sum_{C \in \cc(H)} 
orm{v_C}^2 \E_{x_{-C}} \left[ 
orm{\Cov(\mu_{M_C, h(x_{-C})})_{S}}ight]\mper 	ag*{(	ext{$v$ supported on $S$})} \end{align*} Recall that $M_H =  rac{ eta}{\sqrt{d}}(\ol{A}_H \circ A_H)$. Since the centering interaction is $O( rac{d}{n})$, it follows that the interactions are in $\left[ - rac{ eta}{\sqrt{d}} ,  rac{ eta}{\sqrt{d}} ight] = \left[ -  rac{ eta\sqrt{1+\eps}}{\sqrt{d(1+\eps)}} ,  rac{ eta\sqrt{1+\eps}}{\sqrt{d(1+\eps)}} ight]$, so we can apply \Cref{lem:near-forest-cov} with $\gamma =  eta\sqrt{1+\eps}$. Taking $S = B$ and applying \Cref{lem:near-forest-cov} uniformly on each component $C$ yields  egin{align*} 
orm{\Cov(\mu_{M_R, h'})_B} \le c(1+o(1)) \cdot \left( rac{e^{2 eta/\sqrt{d}}}{(1- eta\sqrt{1+\eps})^2}ight), \end{align*} and for $S = H$ we obtain that  egin{align*} 
orm{\Cov(\mu_{M_R, h'})_H} \le c(1+o(1)) \cdot \left( rac{e^{2 eta/\sqrt{d}}}{(1- eta\sqrt{1+\eps})^2} ight) \cdot  rac{\Delta}{D} . \end{align*} By following the same calculations as in \Cref{thm:main-mixing}, the upshot is that for sufficiently small (constant) $ eta > 0$, we have for some constant $C$ independent of $\Delta$ that  egin{align*} \Cov(\mu_{(1-t)J_B + J_R, h}) \psdle C \left( I_B + \Delta I_{H \setminus \partial H} ight), \end{align*} as desired.

To prove this statement, as in the simpler proof given in the main text, we will use \Cref{lem:entropic-stability,thm:anneal}, albeit with the stochastic localization using a time-varying control matrix. Again, set $s_0 =  rac{\gamma}{\sqrt{D}}$. Consider the graph $H_t$ on $V(H)$ with edge set \[ E(H_t) = \left\{ ij \in E(H) : J_{ij} > t \cdot  rac{\gamma}{\sqrt{D}} ight\}. \] In particular, $H_1$ is empty. The control matrix at time $t$ is defined by $C_t = \left((1-s_0^2) \cdot \BH_{H_t} \left( -  rac{\gamma}{\sqrt{D}} ight)ight)^{1/2}$. As in the simpler proof, this is a non-negative matrix that is well-defined due to \Cref{cor:bethe-tree-psd}. At time $t$, the interaction matrix $J_t$ is, on the off-diagonal elements, supported on the graph $H_t$, with the interactions being at most $ rac{\gamma}{\sqrt{D}}$. In particular, $J_1$ is a diagonal matrix so $\mu_{1}$ is a product measure. Now, for an arbitrary tilt $h$, \[ \Cov\left( \mu_{J_t,h} ight) \le \sum_{\ell \ge 0} \left(  rac{\gamma}{\sqrt{D}} ight)^{\ell} A^{(\ell)}_{H_t} \le \sum_{\ell \ge 0} \left(  rac{\gamma}{\sqrt{D}} ight)^{\ell} A^{(\ell)}_{H} = \BH_{H}(s_0)^{-1}, \] where the inequalities mean that the entries of the (non-negative) matrix on the left are at most that of the corresponding entries on the right. Similarly, because $H_t$ is a subgraph of $H$, $C_t^2 \le (1-s_0^2) \BH_H(-s_0)$. Using \Cref{cor:cov-ferro-external-psd,lem:tree-cov}, we have that  egin{align*} 
orm*{ C_t \cdot \Cov\left( \mu_{J_t,h} ight) \cdot C_t } &\le 
orm*{ \Cov\left( \mu_{J_t,h} ight) \cdot C_t^2 } \ &\le (1-s_0^2) 
orm*{ \BH_{H}(s_0)^{-1} \cdot \BH_{H}(-s_0) } \ &= (1-s_0^2) 
orm*{ \BH_{H}(s_0)^{-1} \cdot \left(\BH_{H}(s_0) +  rac{2s_0}{1-s_0^2} \cdot A_{H} ight) } \ &\le (1-s_0^2) +  rac{2s_0}{1-s_0^2} 
orm*{ \BH_{H}(s_0)^{-1} \cdot A_{H} } \ &\le 1 +  rac{8}{(1-\gamma)^2} \cdot  rac{\Delta}{D}. \end{align*} The measure at time $1$ is almost surely a product measure, therefore  egin{align*} \MLSI(\mu_{J,h}) &\ge  rac{1}{n} \cdot \exp \left( - \int_0^1 \sup_{h \in \R^n} \left\| C_t \cdot \Cov\left( \mu_{J_t,h} ight) \cdot C_t ight\| ight) \ &\ge  rac{1}{n} \cdot \exp\left( - \left( 1 +  rac{8}{(1-\gamma)^2} \cdot  rac{\Delta}{D} ight) ight) =  rac{1}{ne^{O(\Delta)}}. \qedhere \end{align*}

Using \Cref{cor:cov-ferro-external-psd,lem:tree-cov}, we have that  egin{align*} 
orm*{ C_t \cdot \Cov\left( \mu_{J_t,h} ight) \cdot C_t } &\le 
orm*{ \Cov\left( \mu_{J_t,h} ight) \cdot C_t^2 } \ &\le (1-s_0^2) 
orm*{ \BH_{H}(s_0)^{-1} \cdot \BH_{H}(-s_0) } \ &= (1-s_0^2) 
orm*{ \BH_{H}(s_0)^{-1} \cdot \left(\BH_{H}(s_0) +  rac{2s_0}{1-s_0^2} \cdot A_{H} ight) } \ &\le (1-s_0^2) +  rac{2s_0}{1-s_0^2} 
orm*{ \BH_{H}(s_0)^{-1} \cdot A_{H} } \ &\le 1 +  rac{8}{(1-\gamma)^2} \cdot  rac{\Delta}{D}. \end{align*}
